    "message": {
     "content": "### The Verdict: **Do Not Accept as-is \u2014 Negotiate**\n\nYou are a happy, passive candidate with strong leverage. While this is a reasonable **initial baseline offer** for a Google L6 PM, it is **not compelling enough to leave Meta as-is**:\n\n* **Base Salary Decrease:** Your base drops from **$240k to $235k**.\n* **Modest Annualized Increase:** On a straight 4-year average, the Google offer is **~$552,000/yr** ($235k base + $47k bonus + $270k/yr stock). Compared to your current **$526,000/yr**, that is only about a **5% total compensation increase**.\n* **Missing Sign-on Bonus:** Leaving a team where you are performing well and have established social capital warrants a transition buffer. $0 sign-on is standard for Google\u2019s opening anchor, but highly negotiable.\n\n---\n\n### Key Financial & Structural Nuances to Understand\n\n#### 1. Google\u2019s Front-Loaded Equity\nGoogle typically does not vest equity evenly at 25% per year. Instead, they use a front-loaded schedule (commonly **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** or **38% / 32% / 20% / 10%**):\n* **Years 1 & 2:** You will see a strong initial cash flow (approx. **$638k\u2013$690k TC** in Year 1).\n* **Years 3 & 4:** The initial grant drops off sharply (to ~$237k and ~$130k in stock value). You rely on annual refreshes to bridge that \"cliff.\"\n\n#### 2. The Recruiter\u2019s Refresh Comment\nWhile stacked refreshes are a real mechanism at Google, they are tied to performance ratings and budget cycles. You should never price unvested/hypothetical refreshes into your base decision to switch jobs.\n\n---\n\n### What to Ask For (Your Counter-Offer Targets)\n\nBecause Google initiated contact and you are happy where you are, you have maximum leverage. Here is what top-of-band Google L6 PM compensation looks like and what you should target:\n\n| Component | Google Initial Offer | Your Counter Target | Rationale |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$255,000 \u2013 $260,000** | Must beat your $240k Meta base; fits inside their posted $183k\u2013$271k band. |\n| **Equity (4-yr GSU)** | $1,080,000 ($270k/yr avg) | **$1,300,000 \u2013 $1,400,000** ($325k\u2013$350k/yr avg) | Pushes your 4-year average TC above $620k/yr. |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | $0 | **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** | Standard L6 signing bridge to offset unvested Meta RSUs/tenure risk. |\n| **Target Year 1 TC** | ~$638,000 (front-loaded) | **~$750,000+** | Significantly beats your current trajectory. |\n\n---\n\n### Negotiation Strategy & Talking Points\n\n#### How to Frame It:\n1. **Show excitement about the scope/team:** Express that you enjoyed meeting the team and are intrigued by the product challenge.\n2. **Anchor to your Meta reality:** Explicitly state that moving laterally with a base pay cut and a ~5% annualized TC increase does not make financial sense given your strong standing and equity trajectory at Meta.\n3. **Give clear numbers:** Google recruiters prefer specific numbers they can take directly to the compensation committee (Comp Comm).\n\n#### Sample Script for the Recruiter:\n\n> *\"Thank you for putting this offer together. I really enjoyed meeting the team, and I\u2019m genuinely excited about the scope of the role.*\n> \n> *However, as we discussed, I wasn\u2019t actively looking and I\u2019m in a very strong position at Meta making $526k with an established track record. Looking at the annualized numbers, this offer represents a slight decrease in my base salary and only a marginal ~5% bump in four-year average total compensation, without any transition buffer.*\n> \n> *To walk away from my current trajectory and make this move financially compelling, I would need:*\n> * *A base salary of **$255,000** (to reflect an increase over my current $240k base),*\n> * *A 4-year equity grant of **$1.35M**, and*\n> * *A sign-on bonus of **$75,000**.*\n> \n> *If we can get to these numbers, I\u2019d be thrilled to sign and join the team.\"*\n\n---\n\n### Next Steps Summary\n* **Do not sign the current offer.**\n* **Present the counter-offer confidently.** Google rarely rescinds offers for professional, data-backed negotiations.\n* **If they meet you near the target ($600k+ avg TC + sign-on):** Accept.\n* **If they refuse to budge:** Walk away and stay at Meta knowing your market value is strong.",
