     "content": "### The Verdict: **Yes, this is a very strong offer. You should take it (or use it for a retention counter at Meta).**\n\nNegotiating a **$200,000 sign-on bonus** alongside matching your Meta base is a major win. Getting Google\u2019s Compensation Committee to approve a $200k signing bonus without a competing active interview in play is rare and represents the top tier of their L6 discretionary budget.\n\n---\n\n### The Financial Breakdown\n\nBecause of how Google\u2019s front-loaded vesting (standard **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%**) pairs with the $200k sign-on, your near-term earnings jump significantly:\n\n| Component | Year 1 | Year 2 | Year 3 | Year 4 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $240,000 | $240,000 | $240,000 |\n| **Target Bonus (20%)** | $48,000 | $48,000 | $48,000 | $48,000 |\n| **Initial GSU Vest** | ~$356,400 *(33%)* | ~$356,400 *(33%)* | ~$237,600 *(22%)* | ~$129,600 *(12%)* |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | $200,000 | $0 | $0 | $0 |\n| **Total Realized Comp** | **~$844,400** | **~$644,400** | **~$525,600** *(+refreshes)* | **~$417,600** *(+refreshes)* |\n\n* **4-Year Total Compensation:** **~$2.43M** (an average of **~$608,000/year**).\n* **Comparison to Meta:** At your current Meta trajectory (~$526k/year), you would make ~$2.10M over 4 years. Google is paying you **+$328,000 more over the 4-year cycle**, heavily front-loaded into Years 1 and 2.\n* **The \"Unvested Stock\" Math:** The $200k sign-on completely replaces the unvested Meta RSUs you walk away from, while still giving you Google's Year 1 stock vest (~$356k) on top of it.\n\n---\n\n### Three Crucial Details to Check Before Signing\n\n1. **Sign-On Bonus Terms:** \n   * Is it paid out 100% in your first 30\u201360 days, or split into two $100k installments (Year 1 and Year 2)?\n   * What is the clawback period? (Standard is 12 months prorated).\n2. **Vesting Schedule Confirmation:** \n   * Confirm whether your grant follows the front-loaded schedule (**33/33/22/12**) or an even 4-year schedule (**25/25/25/25**).\n3. **Start Date Timing:**\n   * Look at Meta's next quarterly vest date (e.g., February / May / August / November 15th). If you are close to a vesting date, time your Google start date to capture that Meta vest *before* you leave, further maximizing your transition cash.\n\n---\n\n### Your Final Decision Paths\n\n#### Option A: Accept Google\n* **Why:** You get an **~$844k Year 1**, diversify your resume across both premier Big Tech PM organizations, and enter Google at the top of the L6 pay band.\n* **What to expect:** Google PM culture requires more consensus-building and stakeholder alignment compared to Meta\u2019s bottom-up, move-fast execution culture.\n\n#### Option B: Stay at Meta via a Retention Counter\n* If you genuinely love your current team and do not want to leave, you can show this Google offer letter to your Meta VP/HR to request an **off-cycle retention equity grant**.\n* Meta frequently matches or provides retention grants for top performers holding competing Big Tech L6 offers.\n\n**Recommendation:** Accept the Google offer if you are excited about the team and product area. From a compensation standpoint, the recruiter is not bluffing\u2014this is at the ceiling for an L6 PM initial entry.",
